 R_crit the central quantitative parameter in the community. [Data: Entities (373, 400, 350, 111); Relationships (624, 700, 801, 775)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Nb steel shows strongly retarded recrystallization and difficult hot rolling\",\"explanation\":\"NB STEEL is a niobium steel whose recrystallization behavior is strongly affected by Nb. Nb in solution delays gamma-recrystallization, which promotes grain refinement. Strain-induced precipitation of fine Nb(C,N) pins dislocations, causing a severe conflict between dislocation hardening and separation/softening and making Nb steel difficult to hot roll in the steady-state region. As a result, Nb steel has a much larger critical reduction for recrystallization and a substantially higher temperature dependence than plain carbon steel; the grain-size effect on R_crit is also extremely large, and the critical strain for steady state is very large. Overall recrystallization behavior is generally retarded. [Data: Entities (111, 438, 373, 371); Relationships (775, 539, 627)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Plain C steel has small R_crit and low temperature dependence\",\"explanation\":\"PLAIN C STEEL is a carbon steel that does not contain Nb. Its critical amount of deformation, R_crit, required for completion of recrystallization is small, and the temperature dependence of R_crit is low. Recrystallization becomes sluggish at low temperatures in the gamma-region, around 900C, approximately Ar3. The entity C STEEL is also referred to as plain C steel. These features provide the baseline against which Nb steel behavior is contrasted. [Data: Entities (350, 492, 373, 371); Relationships (801, 888, 627)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Deformation temperature controls grain structure and recrystallization regions\",\"explanation\":\"DEFORMATION TEMPERATURE is the temperature at which deformation is applied and is a key factor affecting changes in grain structure. It influences recrystallized grain size, R_crit, and recrystallization regions. Lower deformation temperature tends to produce smaller recrystallized grain sizes, although this effect is slight. The transition temperature decreases with decreasing deformation temperature, and reports on its variation in the two-phase region disagree. Deformation temperature also affects deformation-band density in the non-recrystallization region and above approximately 1000C. A recommended deformation interval is 1000-850C, and behavior above about 1000C is also considered. [Data: Entities (181, 402, 1456, 371); Relationships (239, 683, 3048)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Temperature dependence differentiates Nb steel from plain C steel\",\"explanation\":\"TEMPERATURE DEPENDENCE refers to how R_crit changes with temperature. This dependence is small in plain C steel and substantially higher in Nb steel. Specifically, the temperature dependence of the critical reduction required for recrystallization is much larger for Nb steel than for plain C steel, and the relationship record confirms that in plain C steel the temperature dependence is small. This contrast is a key differentiator in the community. [Data: Entities (371, 373, 350, 111); Relationships (627, 775)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Fine Nb(C,N) precipitation is a mechanism for larger critical reduction\",\"explanation\":\"FINE NB(C, N) is a fine strain-induced precipitate considered responsible for the larger critical reduction and temperature dependence in Nb steel. The relationship record states that strain-induced precipitation of fine Nb(C,N) is considered responsible for the larger critical amount of reduction in Nb steel. This mechanism explains why Nb steel requires much more deformation to complete recrystallization than plain C steel and why its temperature dependence is higher. [Data: Entities (438, 111, 371, 373); Relationships (775)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Lower finish rolling temperatures improve Nb steel toughness\",\"explanation\":\"The improvement in toughness was much greater for Nb steel than for Nb-free steel when finish roll